  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday night through Tuesday)\nIssued at 116 AM MST Wed Nov 11 2026\n\nFriday begins the long term period with continued tranquil weather \nwith an encroaching change to the weather pattern from the west. \nThis changing weather pattern will feature two upper level troughs \ntraversing west to east over the region, bringing unsettled weather \nthis weekend. Numerical model guidance remains in good agreement \nthat the 1st system will take a southerly track along the \ninternational border with rain showers favoring the southern third \nof NM or so. The 2nd system will take a more northerly track across \nthe state, favoring the NM/CO border. This system looks to bear a 90-\n100kt H3 jetmax along its southern flank, with the ECMWF and GFS \nboth resolving this system undergoing cyclogenesis and closing off \nto an H5 low. Differences remain in the solutions with when this \nwill occur however, ranging from over northern AZ, to just lee of \nthe Rocky Mts. Nevertheless, valley rain and mountain snow (Snow \nLevels favoring 8,500' - 9,500') will favor the northern third to \nhalf of NM Saturday afternoon. The 1st system will also have helped \nadvecting northward some lower level moisture from the Gulf up along \nthe NM/TX border where some afternoon/evening shower activity will \nalso be present. A quick and healthy round of 0.25\" to 0.50\" of \nprecipitation will favor west facing slopes of the northwestern \nmountains during this time. Despite these relatively modest \nprecipitation amounts, the still sensitive and very soaked ground \nconditions present across northwestern NM may still allow for minor \nflooding concerns there. Current probabilities of 25% to 45% for \n0.50\" of precipitation or higher favors the higher elevations of the \nCO San Juan Mts where orographically enhanced precipitation will \nlikely fall as snow. \n\nSaturday night into Sunday morning will feature lee cyclogenesis \nacting to strengthen the H5 low as it quickly ejects eastward over \nnortheastern NM. With aforementioned advection northward of 45F-50F \nTd's up the TX Panhandle into eastern NM, these factors will combine \nto allow for a brief period of some robust showers and perhaps a \nthunderstorm or two over northeastern and east-central NM Saturday \nnight into Sunday morning. Precipitation clears out of northeastern \nNM last by Sunday afternoon. \n\nRegarding winds, gusty westerlies of 35-45mph are favored over the \nhigher terrain of western and central NM. H7 winds of about that \nmagnitude are currently being modeled with this system, and the \naccompanying surface low over eastern NM is not very deep. Gusty \nnortherly winds will fill in behind this system through northeastern \nNM, competing with westerlies south of I-40 Sunday afternoon. This \nwill create some marginally hazardous crosswinds on area highways \nthru eastern NM during this time. \n\nAfter a brief intermission Monday seeing clear and tranquil weather \nconditions over the Land of Enchantment, the next Pacific storm \nsystem in the series approaches the region Tuesday and Wednesday. \nAmongst global numerical model suites, there exists a split between \nthis next H5 low opening and ejecting northeastward over UT/CO and a \ndeeper trough lasting longer over the Desert Southwest before \nlifting northward. The GEFS suite currently favors the former by \nroughly half of its members, with the Canadian GEPS suite in the \nlatter's camp. Both scenarios favor another round of precipitation \nover the northwestern third to half of NM, favoring west facing \nslopes of the northern mountains Tuesday and Wednesday.",
